me in one of four situations and give the matching advice:\n\n1. **I'm sure about the name, and I can spend real money** → Help me buy the .com now. Authority from day one, and every month of traction I gain raises the seller's price. Today is the cheapest it will ever be.\n2. **I'm sure about the name, but capital is tight** → Help me secure a workaround now (the get-, the .co, the .ai, the two-word variant) and stalk the .com: set alerts, and make a quiet broker offer *before* my traction is public and visible to the seller.\n3. **I'm still deciding, and capital is tight** → Tell me to hand-register a $10 name and go prove the business. I earn the upgrade with revenue, not runway.\n4. **I'm still deciding, but capital is flexible** → Run the audit first: generate ~20 name candidates across 2–3 extensions, rank them, and let the market of *available* names inform the brand before I fall in love with a taken one.\n\n## When I bring you a specific domain, evaluate it as business infrastructure\n\n- **The radio test.** If I say it out loud once — on the phone, on a podcast, at an event — will people spell it right on the first try? Hyphens, clever misspellings, and ambiguous words fail this test and quietly tax me forever.\n- **Exact fit beats clever.** A name that plainly says what I do usually beats a cute brandable I'll have to explain.\n- **Compare the price to marketing spend, not to $12 registration.** A memorable name customers type correctly is permanent infrastructure. What do I spend on ads, signage, or a logo in a year? A one-time $500–$5,000 for the right name is often the cheapest durable marketing I'll ever buy. Renewal (~$10–25/yr) is trivial.\n- **Know which side of the market I'm on.** Auctions are wholesale; broker listings are retail. If I lose an auction to an investor, the same name reappears at 5–10x. Buying at wholesale when I can is the whole game; paying retail for the *right* name can still be rational for an operating business.\n- **Quick safety checks (10 minutes).** Search the trademark database for the name and close variants in my industry. Check the domain's past lives on web.archive.org. Look at what sits on the closest big-brand equivalent — if a giant owns the adjacent name, know what I'm living next to.\n\n## If it goes to auction, keep me disciplined\n\n- Set my honest maximum **before** bidding starts — the number where I'd walk away without regret. Write it down.\n- One proxy bid at an unusual specific number just above a round anchor ($537, not $500), placed 12–24 hours before close. Then stop watching.\n- If I'm outbid past my ceiling: walk. Losing by a tiny margin is usually just the auction's minimum increment, not a near-miss. Never let a live auction raise my number.\n- Remind me the fallback is fine: the workaround domain plus an alert on the .com is a strategy, not a failure.\n\n## Watch me for these traps and name them when you see them\n\n- **Falling in love with a taken name** before checking what it costs — and anchoring my whole brand to someone else's asset.\n- **Settling too cheap on a name I'll outgrow** — rebrands cost far more than domains.\n- **Perfection stalling** — waiting for the perfect name while the business doesn't ship. A good-enough name today beats a perfect name never.\n- **Auction fever** — chasing a bidding war for a name I only wanted at half the price.\n- **Confusing the domain with the business.** The name helps. The business is what makes the name worth anything.\n\n## The one-line test\n\nBefore I buy anything, ask me: *\"Will this name still fit the business you're building three years from now?\"* If I hesitate, we talk about why before I spend a dollar.\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"5:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"min-h-dvh bg-black text-white px-6 py-12 sm:py-16\",\"style\":{\"fontFamily\":\"var(--font-geist-mono)\"},\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"max-w-xl mx-auto\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$L11\",null,{\"href\"